    "system": "You are sitting a PhD-level qualifying examination in statistics. Solve the problem rigorously and completely. Show your work and reasoning, justify each step, and clearly state your final answer(s). If the problem has multiple parts, answer every part, labeled. If you invoke a known theorem, name it.",
    "user": "[2024 summer phd probability — problem 4]\n\nPart II. Let $(V, E)$ be a finite undirected graph, $V = \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$, with $v \\sim v'$ iff $\\{v, v'\\} \\in E$. State space $\\mathcal{X} = \\{-1, 1\\}^n$ (spin configurations). Define $H(x) = \\sum_{\\{v,v'\\}\\in E}\\mathbb{1}\\{x(v) \\ne x(v')\\} = \\tfrac12\\sum_{\\{v,v'\\}\\in E}(1 - x(v)x(v'))$ and $\\pi(x) = \\frac{e^{-H(x)}}{\\sum_y e^{-H(y)}}$. The update $X_{t+1} = f_{t+1}(x_t)$: sample $v_{t+1} \\sim \\mathrm{Unif}(V)$, $S_{t+1} \\sim \\mathrm{Unif}\\{-1,1\\}$, $U_{t+1} \\sim \\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]$; propose $y$ with $y(v_{t+1}) = S_{t+1}$, $y(w) = x_t(w)$ otherwise; accept ($f_{t+1}(x_t) = y$) if $U_{t+1} \\le \\min\\{1, e^{-H(y)}/e^{-H(x_t)}\\}$, else reject. $P(x, z) = P(f_{t+1}(x) = z)$.\n(1) Compute $P(x, z)$ for $z \\ne x$ with $P(x, z) > 0$ (need not compute $P(x, x)$).\n(2) Prove $\\pi$ is stationary for this chain.\n(3) With $N_{t+1} \\subset V$ the neighbors of $v_{t+1}$ and $y$ the proposed move, show $\\frac{e^{-H(y)}}{e^{-H(x_t)}} = \\exp(\\tfrac12\\sum_{w \\in N_{t+1}}(S_{t+1} - x_t(v_{t+1})) x_t(w))$.\n(4) Write $x \\preceq y$ if $x(v) \\le y(v)$ for all $v$. Prove that if $x \\preceq z$ then $P(f_{t+1}(x) \\preceq f_{t+1}(z)) = 1$ (handle $S_{t+1} = \\pm1$ separately) — i.e. the dynamics is monotone.\n(5) Let $\\underline x = (-1, \\dots, -1)$, $\\bar x = (1, \\dots, 1)$. Prove $F = f_t \\circ \\cdots \\circ f_1$ is constant iff $F(\\underline x) = F(\\bar x)$. Let $T = \\min\\{t \\ge 1 : f_t \\circ \\cdots \\circ f_1(\\underline x) = f_t \\circ \\cdots \\circ f_1(\\bar x)\\}$.\n(6) For $z \\in \\mathcal{X}$, let $h(z)$ be the max number $k$ of distinct states $x_1 \\preceq \\cdots \\preceq x_k = z$. Prove that if $x_t \\preceq y_t$ and $x_t \\ne y_t$ then $h(x_t) + 1 \\le h(y_t)$.\n(7) With $X_t = f_t \\circ \\cdots \\circ f_1(\\underline x)$, $Y_t = f_t \\circ \\cdots \\circ f_1(\\bar x)$ (so $\\{T > t\\} = \\{X_t \\ne Y_t\\}$), prove $P(T > t) \\le \\mathbb{E}[h(Y_t) - h(X_t)]$.\n(8) Prove $0 \\le h(z) \\le |V|$ and $\\mathbb{E}[h(Y_t) - h(X_t)] \\le |V|\\,\\bar d(t)$.\n(9) If $|V| = 2L$ ($L > 1$ integer) and $k \\ge 0$, prove $P(T > t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(k + L)) \\le \\frac{1}{2^k}$ (state the properties of $d, \\bar d, t_{\\mathrm{mix}}$ used).\n(10) Prove $\\mathbb{E}[T] \\le c\\, L\\, t_{\\mathrm{mix}}$ for an absolute constant $c > 0$ (you may first show any nonnegative $U$ with $P(U > k) \\le 1/2^k$ for all integers $k \\ge 0$ has finite expectation).",
