    "text": "# Solution\n\nThroughout, write $n = |V|$.\n\n## (1) Transition probabilities for $z \\neq x$\n\nFor $z \\neq x$ with $P(x,z) > 0$, $z$ must differ from $x$ at exactly one vertex $v$, with $z(v) = -x(v)$. The proposal yields $z$ iff $v_{t+1} = v$ and $S_{t+1} = z(v)$; then acceptance happens with probability $\\min\\{1, e^{H(x)-H(z)}\\}$:\n$$\\boxed{P(x,z) = \\frac{1}{2n}\\min\\!\\left\\{1,\\, e^{H(x)-H(z)}\\right\\}}.$$\n\n## (2) Stationarity of $\\pi$\n\nFor $x,z$ differing at one vertex (the only nontrivial case),\n$$\\pi(x)P(x,z) = \\frac{1}{2nZ}\\min\\{e^{-H(x)},e^{-H(z)}\\} = \\pi(z)P(z,x).$$\nHence $\\pi$ is reversible, so $\\sum_x \\pi(x)P(x,z) = \\sum_x \\pi(z)P(z,x) = \\pi(z)$. $\\square$\n\n## (3) Local energy formula\n\nOnly edges incident to $v_{t+1}$ contribute to $H(y)-H(x_t)$:\n$$H(y) - H(x_t) = \\tfrac12\\sum_{w\\in N_{t+1}}\\big(x_t(v_{t+1})x_t(w) - S_{t+1}\\,x_t(w)\\big) = \\tfrac12\\sum_{w\\in N_{t+1}}(x_t(v_{t+1})-S_{t+1})\\,x_t(w).$$\nNegating gives the claim. $\\square$\n\n## (4) Monotonicity of the coupling\n\nUse common $v = v_{t+1}$, $S = S_{t+1}$, $U = U_{t+1}$. For $w \\neq v$, $f(x)(w) = x(w) \\leq z(w) = f(z)(w)$. At $v$:\n\n**Case $S=+1$.** If $z(v) = +1$, then $f(z)(v) = +1 \\geq f(x)(v)$. If $z(v)=-1$, then $x(v)=-1$ too. The acceptance ratios are\n$$r_x = e^{\\sum_{w\\in N(v)} x(w)},\\qquad r_z = e^{\\sum_{w\\in N(v)} z(w)}.$$\nSince $x(w)\\leq z(w)$, $r_x \\leq r_z$, so $z$-rejection implies $x$-rejection. Either both accept (then $f(x)(v)=f(z)(v)=+1$) or both reject (then both $=-1$).\n\n**Case $S=-1$.** Symmetric: if $x(v)=-1$, $f(x)(v)=-1\\leq f(z)(v)$. If $x(v)=+1$, then $z(v)=+1$; with $r_x = e^{-\\sum x(w)} \\geq e^{-\\sum z(w)} = r_z$, $x$-rejection implies $z$-rejection, so we cannot have $f(x)(v)=+1$ while $f(z)(v)=-1$. $\\square$\n\n## (5) Constancy\n\n$(\\Rightarrow)$ Trivial. $(\\Leftarrow)$ For any $x$, $\\underline x \\preceq x \\preceq \\bar x$. By iterating (4), $F(\\underline x) \\preceq F(x) \\preceq F(\\bar x)$. If $F(\\underline x) = F(\\bar x)$, then $F(x) = F(\\underline x)$. $\\square$\n\n## (6) Height grows along $\\prec$\n\nAdopt $h(z) := \\max\\{k \\geq 0:\\ \\exists\\ x_0 \\prec x_1 \\prec \\cdots \\prec x_k = z\\}$ (so $h(z) = \\#\\{v: z(v)=+1\\}$). If $x_t \\preceq y_t$ and $x_t \\neq y_t$, take a maximal chain $x_0\\prec\\cdots\\prec x_k = x_t$ with $k=h(x_t)$. Append $y_t$:\n$$x_0\\prec\\cdots\\prec x_k = x_t \\prec y_t,$$\nso $h(y_t) \\geq k+1 = h(x_t)+1$. $\\square$\n\n## (7) Bound on $P(T>t)$\n\nBy induction with (4), $X_t \\preceq Y_t$ a.s. By (6),\n$$h(Y_t) - h(X_t) \\geq \\mathbb 1\\{X_t \\neq Y_t\\} = \\mathbb 1\\{T>t\\}.$$\nTaking expectations: $P(T>t) \\leq \\mathbb E[h(Y_t) - h(X_t)]$. $\\square$\n\n## (8) Bounds on $h$ and the expected gap\n\nSince $h(z) = \\#\\{v: z(v)=+1\\}$, $0 \\leq h(z) \\leq |V|$. Now\n$$\\mathbb E[h(Y_t)-h(X_t)] = \\sum_{v\\in V}\\big[P(Y_t(v)=+1)-P(X_t(v)=+1)\\big].$$\nFor each $v$, the event $A_v=\\{z: z(v)=+1\\}$ gives\n$$P(Y_t\\in A_v) - P(X_t\\in A_v) \\leq \\|P^t(\\bar x,\\cdot) - P^t(\\underline x,\\cdot)\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}} \\leq \\bar d(t).$$\nSumming over $|V|$ vertices yields $\\mathbb E[h(Y_t)-h(X_t)] \\leq |V|\\,\\bar d(t)$. $\\square$\n\n## (9) Tail bound for $T$\n\n**Facts used:** $d(t) := \\max_x \\|P^t(x,\\cdot)-\\pi\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}$; $\\bar d(t) := \\max_{x,y}\\|P^t(x,\\cdot)-P^t(y,\\cdot)\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}$; submultiplicativity $\\bar d(s+t)\\leq \\bar d(s)\\bar d(t)$; $\\bar d(t) \\leq 2 d(t)$; $d(t_{\\mathrm{mix}}) \\leq 1/4$.\n\nHence $\\bar d(t_{\\mathrm{mix}}) \\leq 1/2$, and $\\bar d(\\ell\\, t_{\\mathrm{mix}}) \\leq 2^{-\\ell}$. Writing $t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(j) = j\\cdot t_{\\mathrm{mix}}$ and combining (7)–(8):\n$$P(T > (L+k)t_{\\mathrm{mix}}) \\leq |V|\\,\\bar d((L+k)t_{\\mathrm{mix}}) \\leq 2L\\cdot 2^{-(L+k)} = \\frac{L}{2^{L-1}}\\cdot 2^{-k}.$$\nFor $L > 1$ one checks $L \\leq 2^{L-1}$ (by induction), so $\\frac{L}{2^{L-1}} \\leq 1$ and\n$$P(T > t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(L+k)) \\leq 2^{-k}. \\qquad\\square$$\n\n## (10) Bound on $\\mathbb E[T]$\n\n**Lemma.** If $U\\geq 0$ and $P(U>k)\\leq 2^{-k}$ for all integers $k\\geq 0$, then \n$$\\mathbb EU = \\int_0^\\infty P(U>t)\\,dt \\leq \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty P(U>k) \\leq \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty 2^{-k} = 2.$$\n\n**Application.** Since $T$ is a nonneg integer,\n$$\\mathbb E T = \\sum_{t=0}^\\infty P(T>t) = \\sum_{t=0}^{L\\,t_{\\mathrm{mix}}-1}P(T>t) + \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\sum_{t=(L+k)t_{\\mathrm{mix}}}^{(L+k+1)t_{\\mathrm{mix}}-1}P(T>t).$$\nThe first piece is $\\leq L\\,t_{\\mathrm{mix}}$. For the second, by (9), each inner sum is at most $t_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\cdot 2^{-k}$. Thus\n$$\\mathbb E T \\leq L\\,t_{\\mathrm{mix}} + t_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty 2^{-k} = (L+2)\\,t_{\\mathrm{mix}} \\leq 3L\\,t_{\\mathrm{mix}}$$\nsince $L\\geq 2$. So with $c = 3$, $\\mathbb E[T] \\leq c\\,L\\,t_{\\mathrm{mix}}$. $\\square$",
